<!-- This preview was initially authored by Hannah Burson at the 2024 Doenet workshop -->

<p>
  We will use an example to investigate whether or not the derivative of a product of two functions is the product of the derivatives of the functions and also whether or not the derivative of a quotient of two functions is the quotient of the derivatives of the functions.
</p>
<p>
    Let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be the functions defined by
    <m>f(t) = 2t^2</m> and <m>g(t) = t^3 + 4t</m>.
</p>

    <ol marker="a.">
      <li sectionWideCheckWork forceIndividualAnswerColoring>
        <p>
          Determine <m>f'(t)</m> and <m>g'(t)</m>.
        </p>
      
        <p>
          <answer name="fprime">
            <label>
              <m> f'(t)=</m>
            </label>
            4t
          </answer>
           </p>
        <p>
          <answer name="gprime">
            <label>
              <m> g'(t)=</m>
            </label>
            3t^2+4
          </answer>
        </p>
        <!-- <p>
             <answer name= "PA_2_3_1_ans1" matchPartial><award> <when> $fprime = 4t and $gprime=3t^2+4</when></award> </answer>
        </p> -->
     
      </li>
    </ol>  
<cascade revealAll>
      <aside>
        <title>b. Differentiating a product of basic functions</title>
        <p>
          Let <m>p(t) = 2t^2 (t^3 + 4t)</m> and observe that <m>p(t) = f(t) \cdot g(t)</m>.
        </p>
      
        <p>
          Rewrite the formula for <m>p</m> by distributing the <m>2t^2</m> term and simplifying.</p>
        <p>
            <answer name= "PA_2_3_1_ans2" symbolicEquality>
              <mathInput name="expandedp">
                <label>
                  <m> p(t)=</m>
                </label>
              </mathInput>
               <award> 
                 <when> $expandedp=2t^5+8t^3 or $expandedp=8t^3+2t^5 </when>
               </award>
             </answer>
        </p>
          <feedback condition="$PA_2_3_1_ans2.responseHasBeenSubmitted and $PA_2_3_1_ans2.creditAchieved!=1">
            Write <m>p(t)</m> in the form <m>p(t) = at^n + bt^m</m> for appropriate numerical values of <m>a
            </m>, <m>b</m>, <m>n</m>, and <m>m</m>.
          </feedback>
     
        <p>
          Now, use your formula from the previous prompt to
          compute <m>p'(t)</m> using the sum and constant multiple rules.
        </p>

        <p>
           <answer name= "PA_2_3_1_ans4"><mathInput name="pprime">
             <label>
              <m> p'(t)=</m>
             </label>
             </mathInput>
               <award> 
                 <when> $pprime=10t^4+24t^2 </when>
               </award> 
           </answer>
        </p>
      
        <p>
          Based on your work in part (a), state an expanded and simplified form of the expression <m>f'(t)\cdot g'(t)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <answer name= "PA_2_3_1_ans2a" symbolicEquality>
               <mathInput name="expandedfPgP">
                  <label>
                    <m> f'(t) \cdot g'(t) = </m>
                  </label>
               </mathInput>
               <award> 
                 <when> $expandedfPgP=12t^3 + 16t or $expandedfPgP=16t + 12t^3 </when>
               </award>
            </answer>
        </p>
        <feedback condition="$PA_2_3_1_ans2a.responseHasBeenSubmitted and $PA_2_3_1_ans2a.creditAchieved!=1">
            Write <m>f'(t) \cdot g'(t)</m> in the form <m>f'(t) \cdot g'(t) = ct^k + dt^l</m> for appropriate numerical values of <m>c</m>, <m>d</m>, <m>k</m>, and <m>l</m>.
        </feedback>
      <p>
          Based on your work above, is the following claim true or false? <m>p'(t) = f'(t) \cdot g'(t)</m>

          <answer><shortDescription>true or false about the relationship of derivatives and quotients</shortDescription>
            <choice>True</choice>
            <choice credit="1">False</choice>
          </answer>
       
        </p>
  
      </aside>


      <aside>
        <title>
          c. Differentiating a quotient of basic functions
        </title>
        <p>
          Let <m>q(t) = \frac{t^3 + 4t}{2t^2}</m> and observe that <m>q(t) = \frac{g(t)}{f(t)}</m>.
        </p>
          <p>
            Rewrite the formula for <m>q</m> by dividing each term in the numerator by the denominator and simplify to write <m>q</m> as a sum of constant multiples of powers of <m>t</m>. 
          </p>
         
          <p>
            <answer name="PA_2_3_1_ans5" symbolicEquality>
              <mathInput name="qt">
                 <label>
                   <m>q(t)=</m>
                 </label>
              </mathInput>
                <award>
                  <when> $qt=1/2t+2t^(-1) or $qt=t/2+2t^(-1) </when>
                </award>
                <award credit="0" name="tdenom">
                  <when> $qt=1/2t+2/t or $qt=t/2+2/t </when>
                </award>
            </answer>
          </p>
                <feedback condition="$tdenom">
                    Rewrite the <m>\frac{2}{t}</m> term in the form <m>2t^a</m>, where <m>a</m> is a number.
                </feedback>
                <hint>
                  How can you rewrite <m>\frac1t</m> in the form <m>t^a</m>, where <m>a</m> is some number?
                </hint>
           
          <p>
            Then, compute <m>q'(t)</m> using the sum and constant multiple rules.
          </p>
        
          <p>
            <answer name= "PA_2_3_1_ans6"><mathInput name="qprime">
              <label>
                <m>q'(t)=</m>
              </label>
            </mathInput><award> <when> $qprime=1/2-2t^(-2) </when></award> </answer>
          </p>

          
        <p>
          Based on your work in part (a), state a simplified form of <m>\frac{g'(t)}{f'(t)}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <answer name= "PA_2_3_1_ans2e" ><mathInput name="expandedgPoverfP">
            <label>
              <m>\frac{g'(t)}{f'(t)} = </m>
            </label>
          </mathInput><award symbolicEquality simplifyOnCompare> <when> $expandedgPoverfP=3/4 t + 1/t</when></award><award credit="0" name="quotnotsimplified"> <when> $expandedgPoverfP=3/4 t + 1/t</when></award> </answer>
             
        </p>
        <feedback condition="$quotnotsimplified">Divide each term in the numerator by the denominator. Write your answer in the form <m>ct^k + dt^l</m> for appropriate numerical values of <m>c</m>, <m>d</m>, <m>k</m>, and <m>l</m>.</feedback>

        <p>
          True or false: <m>q'(t) = \frac{g'(t)}{f'(t)}</m>.
        
          <answer><shortDescription>true or false about the relationship of derivatives and quotients</shortDescription>
  <choice>True</choice>
  <choice credit="1">False</choice></answer>
        </p>
      </aside>
</cascade>
